A||||16||Ken Wittlief||Recall was never done on my '85SE V6 - should I have it done?||||1||
Z||000000||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||09:22 AM||||After reading the stuff that was done on the recall for my car, i know the rubber seal is still on my deck lid front edge, and there are no stickers on my deck lid - and the original owner told me he never took the car in for the recall cause he didnt like the 'piece of krap' they were gonna stick on the manifold.<P>  but it looks like as part of th recall they replaced the exhaust manifolds on '85s, did something with the  crankcase breather tube, and something with the coolant lines by the wheel well.<P>    I have a broken manifold stud on both sides of my engine (I dont know why they were messed with, or if the broke on their own) and I am planning on fixing them someday to get rid of the exhaust leaks.<P>   It looks like I could take the car in to a pontiac dealer and have the recall work done (boy would they be happy to see me!) which would mean I get two new exhaust manifolds, they would most likely have to fix the broken manifold bolts on both sides and return the car with no exhaust leaks.<P>   But Im a little leary they might screw the car up worse than it is - maybe from lack of experience with the car (the recalls were done 13 years ago - most of those mechanics are long gone by now). Or maybe they will mess the car up more than it is just out of spite?<P>  whats the general consences? Take it in and let pontaic take a wack at my car? or fix the exhaust manifolds myself when I get around to it and pull that piece of rubber off the front of the deck lid next time I have it off?<P>   In all fairness to GM, the original owner did turn down the recall request, and the car has 109k miles on it now and has not spontainiously combusted, and if I take it in I would be doing it to get my leaky manifolds fixed, not cause I think the car will catch fire...<P>   what do you think?<P>  maybe if I let them repace the engine mounts at the same time, throw a couple hundred dollars of needed repairs their way at the same time, maybe that would make it a little more fair to the dealer?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||09:24 AM||||Is it possible the exhaust manifold studs broke on their own because the recall was never done? maybe the original manifolds stress the bolts too much? maybe thats why they were replaceing them?||||reg||1
Z||000002||BC-GT||05-24-2001||09:33 AM||||Is it possible he recall work was done improperly? My 85GT has had the recall work done and they left the rubber deck seal on as well. Just a thought. My work was done in Canada though.<P>------------------<BR>Darryl Martin<BR>1985 GT <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/pf251a969c96964ef15691e97e7ac4489/feb64e3a.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||09:40 AM||||Ive looked at the recall pages and the drawings of the heat sheild they were suppose to put on. Im not really sure without taking a good long look at my engine.<P>  If I post some Jpgs of my engine compartment, and the exhaust manifolds specifically, can anyone on here tell me for certain if the work was done or not?<P><BR> But whether the work was done or not is a separate issue, threads get derailed easily on here (hope this doesnt sound rude - I appreciate the observation and the question). Lets assume for now the recall was not done - should I take it in or not???<p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steven||05-24-2001||09:45 AM||||According to what comes in the kit for the recall, they only replace the back side manifold. The link I sent you on an earlier thread gave part numbers, and it only listed one manifold. But hey, one free manifold is better than having to pay to repair/replace you old one.||||reg||1
