Z||000006||rodmcneill||05-24-2001||10:04 AM||||The manifold closest to the rear window.||||reg||1
Z||000007||starfighter007@msn.com||05-24-2001||02:19 PM||||I was under the impression that recalls were ONLY good for upto 8 years after date of manufacture?not sure on that though.now if Im wrong.you becha I would take it to my dealer and have them do the recall and do it properly and I also would hold them accountable if they screwed something up in the process of doing the work that they are required by law to do .I can understand your concearn thats BS.that we should worry that we will be penelized for having them fix something that should have been done right in the first place .Besides the factory pays them to do the recall any ways.and screw the idea of throwing them some work that you may or may not need or that you might be able to do your self just my thoughts goodluck||||reg||8
Z||000008||Ken Wittlief||05-24-2001||02:28 PM||||My take on fairness is that the original owner had the chance to take it in and have it done when the car was only two years old.<P>  it would have been much easier to do the manifold work then, when the car was pretty new. <P>  from the wording on the recall notice it sounds like 'whoever owns the car - and no matter how long it has been - if it hasnt been done the dealer has to do it'. nothing about 8 years on the notice.<P>  Now the car is 16 years old, instead of 2 - and they will have to deal with rusty engine mounts and engine carriage bolts - there is more risk of the intake manifold bolts stripping out - vacuum lines and wires breaking - hoses breaking.<P>  since the whole point of the recall is to prevent the car from catching fire, and it hasnt caught fire in 16 years...<P>  I think Im talking myself out of it here. anyone have any solid reasons why I should have it done? Is the original forward manifold acutally defective, or was it only repalced with a modified one to allow the piece of tin to be attached?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Ken Wittlief (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||05-24-2001||03:52 PM||||Starfighter, vehicle recalls DO NOT expire, except on tires which are normally 90 days. Read the recall stuff in my cave. <A HREF="http://fierocave.shorturl.com" TARGET=_blank>The Ogre's Fiero Cave</A><P>others,<P>If the recall is outstanding, it's up to you wether it's done or not. I've covered this about as well as I can.<P>The important thing is making sure you understand the GM paperwork. This does 2 things. The most important is that you know what the defects are and can decide if they really matter to you. Next is that you can tell if GM actually did anything should you decide to have it repaired.<P>A third effect of understanding the peperwork is that you can ID other cars, but most importantly you know what to watch out for even after recall work is done. Things like keeping why the oil level up in the 84 4 cyl is so important.... (Yes I realise this is a V6 thread... The idea is the same.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||starfighter007@msn.com||05-24-2001||06:33 PM||||Theogre yes know Iam really confused by the way I have lurked around in your cave when nobodys are around and I do enjoy it in there.now I did go to the recall section and then to the national highway and tranportation page and now I forget what the wording to the question was but it had to do with how long the recall is in effect it maybe pretty much for ever but they may be only obligated to do the repair for a period of 8 years I guess that after 8years they wont do the repair for free that is my understanding?you see I have 85 fierov6 that was modified to look like F40 and I dont know if the rear brake recall has been done and if it has not Im also afraid they would not do because the body has been modified.so I guess I really dont understand this recall totally any way I do enjoy the cave||||reg||1
Z||000011||rogergarrison||05-24-2001||08:01 PM||||I had the manifold recall done on mine, they fixed it for free and screwed up everything else, tried to tell me the car was all messed up when I dropped it off. Make sure you have the service manager note that its running fine before they start anything so you wont get burned. Cost me  almost $1,000 to fix what they screwed up.||||reg||1
Z||000012||maryjane||05-24-2001||11:23 PM||||The problem I ran into was the service mgr told me outright that although it needed to be done he had no one that had any experience with Fieros and had only had one or two come in for repair. I figured at first he could find out from GM or Pontiac what to do and how to do it but then thought "I make enough of my own problems, don't need someone else adding to them" Now I'm considering taking it to Abilene.(100miles away) good luck  Don ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Nashco||05-25-2001||03:14 AM||||Sometimes I feel like I'm beating a dead horse, but I'm glad these issues come up.  Check out these posts for some more views and experiences:<P> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010230.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/010230.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006081.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006081.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006503.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/006503.html</A> <P>I recommend you get it done; it's not your fault that the previous owner didn't get the recalls done.  Pontiac made a faulty design, and took on the responsibility to fix every car.  Don't let companies take this lightly, it is a long term consequence; the more that's in it for them to make a better product, the more they'll strive for excellence!  That's how I feel about it..good luck whatever you decide.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000014||thomas_l||05-25-2001||09:26 AM||||I'm kinda with Roger on this one. Although I haven't had the experience he did, I would be looking at two issues:<P>1. Is this something that is life threatening? Could it strand me on the side of the road? Have the specific problems leading to the recall been a problem for you?<P>NOTE: My 87 GT has 166k miles on it and won't be going into Pontiac for anything period. If it's gone that far and 14 years, there ain't a damn thing the Pontiac dealer will do for me to improve this car. But then I probably have better stuff in my own garage to do these repairs, am an experienced tech, don't rely on my Fiero as a daily driver, and if the thing blows up beyond reasonable repair, I'll take the plates off, throw the keys in the seat and leave it.<P>2. How much would it cost to just do the recall work yourself or pay a trusted, non-dealer service shop? Are you willing to pay for or deal with extras? Rusted/stripped/missing bolts aren't going to be included unless you happen to also buy new Pontiacs every year at the same dealer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">.<P>There are many good dealers out there that will do the work right and not screw you. If you do decide to get them to perform old recalls or any other service for that matter - check around first. Just remember that warranty/recall work is paid at a lower labor rate than "customer pay" - meaning the service dept doesn't exactly boost revenue with that work and in some cases is incented to do a fast instead of quality repair job.||||reg||13
Z||000015||Nashco||05-26-2001||02:11 AM||||Just FYI, yes, rusted/stripped/missing bolts are covered under recall work.  If they have to remove it for the recall, they have to pay for it.  The recall actually covers for bolt breakage without any special permission.  If you would like, I could scan the entire ORIGINAL recall (I got it from my dad, it's from a few years ago, and is what the mechanics and service managers actually see).<P>When the recall work was done to my car, they broke off the top half of the EGR valve.  I think the mechanic must've been using it for leverage getting to the forward exhaust manifold, and busted the top half right off; it was covered under the recall because a) the mechanic broke it, not normal usage and b) it was a potential fire hazard after it didn't work properly.<P>Also, the labor rate is no lower than any other job.  Yes, Pontiac pays the dealer less per hour than the dealership would normally get, but the dealership isn't required to pay any of that to Pontiac, so it works out to the same profit margin.  Anyway, the service manager, mechanic, parts man, etc. (all the employees involved) make the same amount of money either way, so don't accept that excuse.<P>Lastly, the dealership you work with will handle the situation differently, so don't believe any blanket statements about quality.  The dealership I took mine to was both slow and poor quality, but I know that if I were to take it to the dealerships where I used to live in Washington, I would get both quick and quality service.  It all depends on the service manager and mechanic involved.<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 05-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||rogergarrison||05-27-2001||07:47 AM||||fyi. they did break 3 studs off, they fixed them under the recall too. still free<BR>||||reg||1
